{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_38","type":"agent_message","text":"Authored the complete intent-turn spine amendment; no IDs were allocated and no statuses changed.\n\nAmended rows (before → after, abridged only for the formerly long rationale cells):\n\n- SN-028\n  - `why`: “...git hooks’ pure-sh parse...3.8 floor...” → “...two sources for one dial can give enforcers different policy values...” — removes spent two-reader premise.\n  - `acceptance`: “bare `[section]` headers, one `key = value` per line...two grammars...” → “Every reader obtains its policy answer from the file’s TOML parse...” — retires the shape contract while retaining one-home, migration, and invalid-value outcomes.\n- SR-031\n  - `rationale`: “...two readings are pinned equal over...adversarial files...” → “A policy answer calculated more than once can diverge...one parse gives every enforcer the same answer.” — corrects the premise and partitions declaration validity to SR-137.\n  - `acceptance_criteria`: “hooks’ and checkers’ answers agree over...file-shape table...” → “...one TOML parse, including a dotted key, an inline table and a multi-line string; none refuses...for its shape...” — states the surviving behavior.\n  - `coincident`: “...shape is a checked contract because two grammars read it...” → “...valid TOML forms beyond a line-oriented convention...” — removes the obsolete delivery claim.\n- SR-137\n  - `title`: “One policy home, with a checked shape” → “One policy home with conflict and validity refusal.”\n  - `requirement`: “...line shape is a checked contract...” → “...single policy declaration home and refuse an ambiguous or invalid declaration...” — preserves one-home, duplicate, and invalid-dial obligations.\n  - `rationale`: “Two grammars read this file...” → “A single declaration home gives the owner one place...” — removes obsolete implementation history.\n  - `acceptance_criteria`: “...dotted key, inline table, multi-line string...refused...” → “...duplicate homes and wrong-typed or out-of-range values are refused...” — keeps only live refusals.\n- LLR-031\n  - `title`: “Shared declared-line parse” → “Shared declared-policy parse.”\n  - `code_symbol`: “`_first_declared_line;...`” → “`_load_process_toml;_policies;read_privacy_review;hook_policy_answer`” — removes deleted textual-reader binding.\n  - `detail`: “single first-non-comment-line reader...” → “All policy enforcers obtain...one parsed representation...” — retains closed-answer and fail-closed behavior.\n- LLR-155\n  - `title`: “...one reader, checked shape...” → “...one parse, declaration checks...”\n  - `code_symbol`: “`process_config/process_shape_findings/...`” → “`process_config/config_conflicts/approval_through/human_holds`” — removes deleted shape checker.\n  - `detail`: “`process_shape_findings` refuses...” → valid TOML forms “are not a refusal condition.” — preserves duplicate/type/range refusal.\n  - `rationale`: “...same reason the shape is checked...” → validation and comparison have “one home.” — removes retired premise.\n  - `test_refs`: “`TC-150;TC-151`” remains unchanged after reconciliation; TC-150 now covers the valid-form exemption.\n- IF-037\n  - `data`: “sections...; one key = value per line” → “TOML tables: attestation, policies, checks, build.” — removes line-shape contract.\n- TC-031\n  - `method`: adversarial cross-reader-shape comparison → each dotted-key, inline-table, and multi-line-string form reaches every reader as the TOML parser reads it, with no shape refusal.\n  - `evidence`: broad old suite evidence → named parser-shape, unreadable, mixed-declaration, and review-posture tests.\n- TC-150\n  - `method`: shell/TOML shape-equivalence test → duplicate, invalid-value, and valid-TOML/no-configuration-refusal test.\n  - `evidence`: broad suite reference → named mixed, type, range, guarded-entry-point, and valid-form configuration tests.\n\nSR-137 is retained, not proposed for retirement: approved SR-137 itself already states the retained one-home, duplicate-declaration, and invalid-dial obligations.\n\nThe SN-028 amendment is a **MEANING** change, not merely CLARITY: it deliberately removes the former enforced line-shape and two-grammar-equality obligation. Its held-rung status remains unchanged.\n\nBuilder-facing bindings:\n\n- Tests to create/reconcile: `test_valid_toml_policy_shapes_reach_every_reader`, `test_an_unreadable_policy_file_is_refused`, `test_an_out_of_range_dial_is_refused_not_defaulted`, `test_every_guarded_entry_point_refuses_a_mixed_config`, and `test_valid_toml_policy_shapes_cause_no_configuration_refusal`.\n- Removed symbol bindings: `process_shape_findings` and `_first_declared_line`; LLR-031 and LLR-155 now bind only surviving symbols.\n\nArms map:\n\n- LLR-031\n  - Every enforcer receives one parsed answer → TC-031’s parser-comparison clause.\n  - Dotted/inline/multiline valid TOML is accepted by every reader → TC-031’s per-form no-shape-refusal clause.\n  - Unreadable/unparseable input is distinct and non-defaulting → TC-031’s unreadable-declaration clause.\n  - Exact reviewer opt-down and legacy fallback conditions → TC-031’s reviewer-posture clause.\n- LLR-155\n  - Duplicate declaration refusal → TC-150’s duplicate-declaration clause.\n  - Wrong-type/out-of-range refusal → TC-150’s invalid-value clause.\n  - Valid TOML syntax is exempt from configuration refusal → TC-150’s valid-form clause.\n  - Approval-level comparison remains covered by existing TC-151.\n\nChecks run:\n\n- `check_trajectory.py --strict` — `check_trajectory: clean (...)`, exit 0.\n- `trace.py --strict` — `Traceability: ... orphans=0 integrity=0 ...`, exit 0.\n- Required pytest command — `76 passed, 1 skipped in 153.18s`, exit 0.\n\nThe strict trajectory check reports expected advisory warnings, including that WI-966 now cites amended SR-031/SR-137; I did not edit the work-item specification because this turn permits registry changes only.\n\nDecomposition perspectives:\n\n- SECURITY: SR-137 and SR-031 retain fail-closed invalid/unreadable policy behavior.\n- FIRST-RUN-ADOPTER: SN-028 keeps migration and duplicate-home refusal guidance.\n- CROSS-PLATFORM: no finding; one TOML parse removes shell-format dependence.\n- MAINTAINER: SR-031, SR-137, LLR-031, and LLR-155 now state enduring reasons rather than retired mechanics.\n- TEST-ENGINEER: TC-031 and TC-150 give direct parser-form and refusal evidence.\n- UX-DESIGNER / UX-ENGINEER / ACCESSIBILITY: no finding; no user-rendered surface changes.\n- PERFORMANCE: no finding; no measurable resource risk introduced.\n- CONSISTENCY: SR-031, SR-137, IF-037, LLRs, and TCs were reconciled to one parser vocabulary.\n- INTEGRITY-RECOVERABILITY: no finding; this changes policy interpretation only, not durable-write recovery.\n- PRODUCT-FITNESS: SN-028 remains the anchor and still expresses the requested one-home outcome."}}
